public_text.9:
The poem is the classic stroke-shape description of the three-box / three-sun figure.

Read off left to right / top to bottom

* 一横 再一横 再一横  – the three top horizontals of the three 日 that are put side by side;
* 一竖 再一竖 再一竖  – the three left uprights of those three 日;
* 一竖在左 一竖在右  – the extreme left-right framing of the block, the left-hand and right-hand uprights of the 日 日 日 group;
* 一竖 再一竖 再一竖  – the three right uprights of the three 日.

That inventory is 日 + 日 + 日, i.e. 晶 jīng ‘bright, crystal’, the familiar “three suns” character, 12 strokes, three squares in a row. 

In the school-book three-bar schematic the same stroke inventory is normally written in the compact 9-stroke form

品 pǐn

three horizontal bars 一横 一横 再一横 held between a left upright and a right upright 一竖在左 一竖在右, the left and right sides being read off in the normal stroke order as 一竖 再一竖 再一竖 … 一竖 再一竖 再一竖. 晶 = 日日日 is the literal three-box realisation of the 品-type three-horizontal/three-vertical frame, and that is the form the riddle is traditionally quoted for.

5.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: The poem is the classic ─│ three-box / three-sun stroke inventory.

* 一横 再一横 再一横 = the three top horizontals of three 日 put side by side;
* 一竖 再一竖 再一竖 = the three left-hand uprights of those three 日;
* 一竖在左 一竖在右 = the extreme left / extreme right framing of the three-column block, i.e. the left–right pair that closes each 日;
* 一竖 再一竖 再一竖 = the three right-hand uprights of the three 日.

That is 日 日 日

晶 jīng ‘bright, crystal’, literally “three suns”, the 12-stroke side-by-side realisation of the three-horizontal/three-vertical frame.

It is the same ─│ inventory that school books teach in the compact stacked shorthand

品 pǐn

three horizontal bars flanked by a left and a right upright, the nine-stroke “taste / item” sign. 晶 is the literal three-日 / three-box form of the poem, 品 is its conventional pedagogical stacked rendering; the two names are used interchangeably for the riddle. The outliers 册 / 帛 / 门 do not reproduce the regular threefold horizontal and threefold vertical repetition.

