3.text:
The four counting groups are the classic pure ─ │ three-three inventory and it is now stable across the group.

* 一横 再一横 再一横 = the three top horizontals of the three-column block;
* 一竖 再一竖 再一竖 = the three left-hand uprights of the three 日;
* 一竖在左 一竖在右 = the extreme left / extreme right framing pair of the whole block;
* 一竖 再一竖 再一竖 = the three right-hand uprights of the three 日.

日 = 一 top + 丨 left + 一 bottom + 丨 right

日 日 日 = 晶 jīng

12 strokes, 6 ─ 6 │, “three suns”, bright / crystal. Each 日 contributes the triad of horizontals it needs and the left / right vertical triads named in the poem, and the explicit “one vertical on the left, one vertical on the right” is the natural outer framing of the horizontal three-column realisation.

品 pǐn is the conventional nine-stroke stacked school-book shorthand for the same ─ │ inventory — three horizontals piled one under the other flanked by a left upright and a right upright — and it is the form under which the riddle is most often quoted. Orthographically explicit the wording with its lateral 再一…再一… succession and the left/right framing cue describes the side-by-side realisation 晶 = 日日日, with 品 as its accepted compact three-bar pedagogical rendering.

册 / 帛 / 门/門 do not keep the regular pure horizontal/vertical three-fold recurrence. I stay with 晶 jīng = 日日日 as the primary literal reading, 品 pǐn acknowledged as the standard stacked synonym.

